hard-code the unicode string for testing.
I've read the FAQ and see that GLFW supports Unicode. I have browse the source code and found this function:
WCHAR* _glfwCreateWideStringFromUTF8Win32(const char* source);
in which it calls MultiByteToWideChar() to convert multi-byte string from the title parameter said above to wchar_t*.
So I have reversed the process by call WideCharToMultiByte() to convert my wchar_t* unicode string to const char* and then pass the result in CreateWindow function or SetWindowTitle function. And I find that pretty annoying even I can warp the process in one function, plus convert an unicode string to multi-byte char string then convert it back to unicode string is a waste of time and resource.
So I think there should be a version of glfwCreateWindow() that takes in wchar_t* as window title parameter. Do this may require the change of _GLFWwndconfig structure, or just simple warp the conversion of wchar_t* to const char* then call the former function. The change is not much, but it lift the weight for user.
GLFWAPI GLFWwindow* glfwCreateWindow(int width, int height, const char* title,
                                     GLFWmonitor* monitor, GLFWwindow* share)
{
    char* newtitle;
    int count;
    GLFWwindow* retWindow;

    // first get the size of the buffer, if you know exactly the size then this is not necessary;
    count = WideCharToMultiByte(CP_UTF8, 0, cl, -1, nullptr, 0, NULL, NULL);
    if (!count)
    {
        _glfwInputErrorWin32(GLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR, "Win32: Failed to convert string from UTF-8");
        return NULL;
    }

    newtitle = (char*)calloc(count, sizeof(char));
    WideCharToMultiByte(CP_UTF8, 0, cl, -1, newtitlet, count, NULL, NULL);

    // call the exactly former function;
    retWindow = glfwCreateWindow(width, height, newtitle, monitor, share);

    // need to free the calloc()-ed pointer;
    free(newtitle);

    return retWindow;
}



